# Ostinato: a short pattern repeated under a tune, its length found, its repeats counted over a
# song, tapped, Pachelbel's ground bass played and counted, a pattern that does not fit the bar, and
# one sung.
lesson music-ostinato v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=7 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A pattern that repeats underneath: ostinato"
  goal "Find, count and keep a short pattern going underneath a tune, as Pachelbel's bass does."

  look {
    scene 36x12 {
      notes bass clef=treble notes=[D4, A3, B3, Fs3, G3, D3, G3, A3] values=[1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1] meter=4 letters=true at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      An ostinato is a short pattern of notes or claps played again and again, under a tune that
      keeps changing. When it is in the bass, it is called a ground bass. Johann Pachelbel, who
      lived from 1653 to 1706, wrote a Canon for three violins over a ground bass of just eight
      notes, drawn here a little higher than he wrote them. The bass plays its eight notes 28 times,
      while the three violins play the same tune one after another above it.
      """
  }
  do {
    show music.ost-length k=2 easy-k=2 hard-k=3
    show music.ost-repeats b=8 p=1 easy-b=4 hard-b=8 hard-p=2
    level easy {
      worked music.ost-total t=3 level=medium
    }
    show music.ost-tap
    show music.ost-total t=4 hard-t=10
    show music.ost-ground
    show music.ost-length k=3 easy-k=3 hard-k=4
    show music.ost-lineup p=2 hard-p=3
    show music.ost-repeats b=12 p=2 easy-b=8 easy-p=1 hard-b=12
    level medium hard {
      show music.ost-total t=5 hard-t=28
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.ost-why r=0 easy-r=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.ost-sing v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "An ostinato is a short pattern played again and again under a tune that changes."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a short pattern repeated underneath a song, an ostinato, holds the music
    together while the tune above it changes, and that its length decides how it lines up with the
    bars. The wrong ideas to watch for are counting a pattern's notes past the point where it starts
    again, counting a half-bar pattern once a bar, and adding when the repeats should be multiplied.
    In the working, look for the child saying the letters aloud and stopping when the start comes
    back. The core finds a pattern's length, counts its repeats over a song and its notes over a
    piece, taps one, plays Pachelbel's ground bass and finds when a pattern lines up with the bar
    again; the two-star asks why composers use one, and the three-star is an ostinato kept going
    under your singing, which you listen to and tick rather than mark.

    The facts are from Wikipedia, "Pachelbel's Canon": written for three violins and basso continuo,
    in D major, at an unknown date between about 1680 and 1706, with 28 statements of the ground
    bass; the eight notes drawn are the roots of its chords as the article gives them, D, A, B, F
    sharp, G, D, G, A, an octave above Pachelbel's bass. Pachelbel's dates are from Wikipedia's list
    of Baroque composers. If the lining up is stuck, clap a three-beat pattern while counting bars
    of four aloud. Off the page, listen to the Canon and hum the bass line under it. This lesson
    meets Japan's 2017 course of study for music, grades 3 and 4, A表現(3)イ(イ): 「音やフレーズのつなげ方や重ね方の特徴」
    (how sounds and phrases are joined and layered), and England's key stage 2 aim to "improvise and
    compose music for a range of purposes".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the patterns are two and three notes long, the songs are four and eight bars with a pattern a bar long, the notes over a piece are worked once in the teacher's pen before four times through, the pattern to line up is two beats long, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the ostinato is clapped."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the patterns are three and four notes long, the pattern is half a bar long, the notes are counted over ten and twenty-eight times through, the pattern to line up is three beats long in bars of four, and the ostinato is two bars long on three notes."
  }
}
